.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/prop-types Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "prop-types [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.com/facebook/prop-types.svg?branch=main)](https://travis-ci.org/facebook/prop-types)"
.PP
Runtime type checking for React props and similar objects\&.

.PP
You can use prop-types to document the intended types of properties passed to components\&. React (and potentially other libraries—see the \fRcheckPropTypes()\fP reference below) will check props passed to your components against those definitions, and warn in development if they don’t match\&.
.SH "Installation"
.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install \-\-save prop\-types
.fi
.PP
.SH "Importing"
.PP
.PP
.nf
import PropTypes from 'prop\-types'; // ES6
var PropTypes = require('prop\-types'); // ES5 with npm
.fi
.PP
.SS "CDN"
If you prefer to exclude \fRprop-types\fP from your application and use it globally via \fRwindow\&.PropTypes\fP, the \fRprop-types\fP package provides single-file distributions, which are hosted on the following CDNs:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBunpkg\fP\fP 
.PP
.nf
<!\-\- development version \-\->
<script src="https://unpkg\&.com/prop\-types@15\&.6/prop\-types\&.js"></script>

<!\-\- production version \-\->
<script src="https://unpkg\&.com/prop\-types@15\&.6/prop\-types\&.min\&.js"></script>

.fi
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR\fBcdnjs\fP\fP 
.PP
.nf
<!\-\- development version \-\->
<script src="https://cdnjs\&.cloudflare\&.com/ajax/libs/prop\-types/15\&.6\&.0/prop\-types\&.js"></script>

<!\-\- production version \-\->
<script src="https://cdnjs\&.cloudflare\&.com/ajax/libs/prop\-types/15\&.6\&.0/prop\-types\&.min\&.js"></script>

.fi
.PP

.PP

.PP
To load a specific version of \fRprop-types\fP replace \fR15\&.6\&.0\fP with the version number\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
PropTypes was originally exposed as part of the React core module, and is commonly used with React components\&. Here is an example of using PropTypes with a React component, which also documents the different validators provided:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop\-types';

class MyComponent extends React\&.Component {
  render() {
    // \&.\&.\&. do things with the props
  }
}

MyComponent\&.propTypes = {
  // You can declare that a prop is a specific JS primitive\&. By default, these
  // are all optional\&.
  optionalArray: PropTypes\&.array,
  optionalBigInt: PropTypes\&.bigint,
  optionalBool: PropTypes\&.bool,
  optionalFunc: PropTypes\&.func,
  optionalNumber: PropTypes\&.number,
  optionalObject: PropTypes\&.object,
  optionalString: PropTypes\&.string,
  optionalSymbol: PropTypes\&.symbol,

  // Anything that can be rendered: numbers, strings, elements or an array
  // (or fragment) containing these types\&.
  // see https://reactjs\&.org/docs/rendering\-elements\&.html for more info
  optionalNode: PropTypes\&.node,

  // A React element (ie\&. <MyComponent />)\&.
  optionalElement: PropTypes\&.element,

  // A React element type (eg\&. MyComponent)\&.
  // a function, string, or "element\-like" object (eg\&. React\&.Fragment, Suspense, etc\&.)
  // see https://github\&.com/facebook/react/blob/HEAD/packages/shared/isValidElementType\&.js
  optionalElementType: PropTypes\&.elementType,

  // You can also declare that a prop is an instance of a class\&. This uses
  // JS's instanceof operator\&.
  optionalMessage: PropTypes\&.instanceOf(Message),

  // You can ensure that your prop is limited to specific values by treating
  // it as an enum\&.
  optionalEnum: PropTypes\&.oneOf(['News', 'Photos']),

  // An object that could be one of many types
  optionalUnion: PropTypes\&.oneOfType([
    PropTypes\&.string,
    PropTypes\&.number,
    PropTypes\&.instanceOf(Message)
  ]),

  // An array of a certain type
  optionalArrayOf: PropTypes\&.arrayOf(PropTypes\&.number),

  // An object with property values of a certain type
  optionalObjectOf: PropTypes\&.objectOf(PropTypes\&.number),

  // You can chain any of the above with `isRequired` to make sure a warning
  // is shown if the prop isn't provided\&.

  // An object taking on a particular shape
  optionalObjectWithShape: PropTypes\&.shape({
    optionalProperty: PropTypes\&.string,
    requiredProperty: PropTypes\&.number\&.isRequired
  }),

  // An object with warnings on extra properties
  optionalObjectWithStrictShape: PropTypes\&.exact({
    optionalProperty: PropTypes\&.string,
    requiredProperty: PropTypes\&.number\&.isRequired
  }),

  requiredFunc: PropTypes\&.func\&.isRequired,

  // A value of any data type
  requiredAny: PropTypes\&.any\&.isRequired,

  // You can also specify a custom validator\&. It should return an Error
  // object if the validation fails\&. Don't `console\&.warn` or throw, as this
  // won't work inside `oneOfType`\&.
  customProp: function(props, propName, componentName) {
    if (!/matchme/\&.test(props[propName])) {
      return new Error(
        'Invalid prop `' + propName + '` supplied to' +
        ' `' + componentName + '`\&. Validation failed\&.'
      );
    }
  },

  // You can also supply a custom validator to `arrayOf` and `objectOf`\&.
  // It should return an Error object if the validation fails\&. The validator
  // will be called for each key in the array or object\&. The first two
  // arguments of the validator are the array or object itself, and the
  // current item's key\&.
  customArrayProp: PropTypes\&.arrayOf(function(propValue, key, componentName, location, propFullName) {
    if (!/matchme/\&.test(propValue[key])) {
      return new Error(
        'Invalid prop `' + propFullName + '` supplied to' +
        ' `' + componentName + '`\&. Validation failed\&.'
      );
    }
  })
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
Refer to the \fRReact documentation\fP for more information\&.
.SH "Migrating from React\&.PropTypes"
.PP
Check out \fRMigrating from React\&.PropTypes\fP for details on how to migrate to \fRprop-types\fP from \fRReact\&.PropTypes\fP\&.

.PP
Note that this blog posts \fBmentions a codemod script that performs the conversion automatically\fP\&.

.PP
There are also important notes below\&.
.SH "How to Depend on This Package?"
.PP
For apps, we recommend putting it in \fRdependencies\fP with a caret range\&. For example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
"dependencies": {
  "prop\-types": "^15\&.5\&.7"
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
For libraries, we \fIalso\fP recommend leaving it in \fRdependencies\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
"dependencies": {
  "prop\-types": "^15\&.5\&.7"
},
"peerDependencies": {
  "react": "^15\&.5\&.0"
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBNote:\fP there are known issues in versions before 15\&.5\&.7 so we recommend using it as the minimal version\&.

.PP
Make sure that the version range uses a caret (\fR^\fP) and thus is broad enough for npm to efficiently deduplicate packages\&.

.PP
For UMD bundles of your components, make sure you \fBdon’t\fP include \fRPropTypes\fP in the build\&. Usually this is done by marking it as an external (the specifics depend on your bundler), just like you do with React\&.
.SH "Compatibility"
.PP
.SS "React 0\&.14"
This package is compatible with \fBReact 0\&.14\&.9\fP\&. Compared to 0\&.14\&.8 (which was released in March of 2016), there are no other changes in 0\&.14\&.9, so it should be a painless upgrade\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
# ATTENTION: Only run this if you still use React 0\&.14!
npm install \-\-save react@^0\&.14\&.9 react\-dom@^0\&.14\&.9
.fi
.PP
.SS "React 15+"
This package is compatible with \fBReact 15\&.3\&.0\fP and higher\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install \-\-save react@^15\&.3\&.0 react\-dom@^15\&.3\&.0
.fi
.PP
.SS "What happens on other React versions?"
It outputs warnings with the message below even though the developer doesn’t do anything wrong\&. Unfortunately there is no solution for this other than updating React to either 15\&.3\&.0 or higher, or 0\&.14\&.9 if you’re using React 0\&.14\&.
.SH "Difference from \fRReact\&.PropTypes\fP: Don’t Call Validator Functions"
.PP
First of all, \fBwhich version of React are you using\fP? You might be seeing this message because a component library has updated to use \fRprop-types\fP package, but your version of React is incompatible with it\&. See the \fBabove section\fP for more details\&.

.PP
Are you using either React 0\&.14\&.9 or a version higher than React 15\&.3\&.0? Read on\&.

.PP
When you migrate components to use the standalone \fRprop-types\fP, \fBall validator functions will start throwing an error if you call them directly\fP\&. This makes sure that nobody relies on them in production code, and it is safe to strip their implementations to optimize the bundle size\&.

.PP
Code like this is still fine:

.PP
.PP
.nf
MyComponent\&.propTypes = {
  myProp: PropTypes\&.bool
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
However, code like this will not work with the \fRprop-types\fP package:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Will not work with `prop\-types` package!
var errorOrNull = PropTypes\&.bool(42, 'myProp', 'MyComponent', 'prop');
.fi
.PP

.PP
It will throw an error:

.PP
.PP
.nf
Calling PropTypes validators directly is not supported by the `prop\-types` package\&.
Use PropTypes\&.checkPropTypes() to call them\&.
.fi
.PP

.PP
(If you see \fBa warning\fP rather than an error with this message, please check the \fBabove section about compatibility\fP\&.)

.PP
This is new behavior, and you will only encounter it when you migrate from \fRReact\&.PropTypes\fP to the \fRprop-types\fP package\&. For the vast majority of components, this doesn’t matter, and if you didn’t see \fRthis warning\fP in your components, your code is safe to migrate\&. This is not a breaking change in React because you are only opting into this change for a component by explicitly changing your imports to use \fRprop-types\fP\&. If you temporarily need the old behavior, you can keep using \fRReact\&.PropTypes\fP until React 16\&.

.PP
\fBIf you absolutely need to trigger the validation manually\fP, call \fRPropTypes\&.checkPropTypes()\fP\&. Unlike the validators themselves, this function is safe to call in production, as it will be replaced by an empty function:

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Works with standalone PropTypes
PropTypes\&.checkPropTypes(MyComponent\&.propTypes, props, 'prop', 'MyComponent');
.fi
.PP
 See below for more info\&.

.PP
\fBIf you DO want to use validation in production\fP, you can choose to use the \fBdevelopment version\fP by importing/requiring \fRprop-types/prop-types\fP instead of \fRprop-types\fP\&.

.PP
\fBYou might also see this error\fP if you’re calling a \fRPropTypes\fP validator from your own custom \fRPropTypes\fP validator\&. In this case, the fix is to make sure that you are passing \fIall\fP of the arguments to the inner function\&. There is a more in-depth explanation of how to fix it \fRon this page\fP\&. Alternatively, you can temporarily keep using \fRReact\&.PropTypes\fP until React 16, as it would still only warn in this case\&.

.PP
If you use a bundler like Browserify or Webpack, don’t forget to \fRfollow these instructions\fP to correctly bundle your application in development or production mode\&. Otherwise you’ll ship unnecessary code to your users\&.
.SH "PropTypes\&.checkPropTypes"
.PP
React will automatically check the propTypes you set on the component, but if you are using PropTypes without React then you may want to manually call \fRPropTypes\&.checkPropTypes\fP, like so:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const myPropTypes = {
  name: PropTypes\&.string,
  age: PropTypes\&.number,
  // \&.\&.\&. define your prop validations
};

const props = {
  name: 'hello', // is valid
  age: 'world', // not valid
};

// Let's say your component is called 'MyComponent'

// Works with standalone PropTypes
PropTypes\&.checkPropTypes(myPropTypes, props, 'prop', 'MyComponent');
// This will warn as follows:
// Warning: Failed prop type: Invalid prop `age` of type `string` supplied to
// `MyComponent`, expected `number`\&.
.fi
.PP
.SH "PropTypes\&.resetWarningCache()"
.PP
\fRPropTypes\&.checkPropTypes(\&.\&.\&.)\fP only \fRconsole\&.error\fPs a given message once\&. To reset the error warning cache in tests, call \fRPropTypes\&.resetWarningCache()\fP
